Southwark Council Electoral Services

Thank you for contacting the Electoral Services office at the London Boroug=
h of Southwark.

If your query is answered by this automated reply, you may not receive a fu=
rther direct response. Otherwise, we aim to reply within 10 working days.

You can find further information on our website https://www.southwark.gov.u=
k/council-and-democracy/voting-and-elections

If your enquiry is urgent, please call the Southwark Electoral Services hel=
pline on 020 7525 7373 as soon as possible.

Annual Electoral Canvass 2026

By law, we are required to contact every residential property in Southwark =
each year to confirm that all eligible residents at each address are regist=
ered to vote.

The information we receive is used to update the electoral register by invi=
ting unregistered residents to register to vote and removing the names of e=
lectors who no longer live at the address.

Additions and amendments made to the register during the canvass will take =
effect upon publication of the revised Register of Electors on 1 December 2=
026.

From July to November 2026, we will be contacting households using differen=
t methods. As a result, not all residents will receive communications from =
us at the same time.


  *   You may receive emails and/or text messages from =91Southwark Elector=
al Services=92
  *   You may receive communications by post
  *   We may also contact you by telephone or in person


How do I respond?

To confirm or update the details for your household:


  *   Visit householdresponse.southwark.gov.uk<https://householdresponse.so=
uthwark.gov.uk/> and enter both parts of the security code that is on the c=
orrespondence you have received.


If you have no changes to make, you can also respond by:


  *   Texting NOCHANGE followed by your security codes to 07893 939 113, or=
;
  *   Calling 07893 939 113 and when prompted enter part 1 and part 2 of yo=
ur security code. =96 is this still correct


Please respond straightaway. If we don=92t receive a prompt response, we ar=
e required by law to send reminders to obtain up to date information.

We are using emails and texts extensively, to reduce the costs of obtaining=
 information from all our properties. Please help us by responding to our r=
equest for information as quickly as possible.

If you have any questions about how to respond to the form, please email el=
ectoral.enquiries@southwark.gov.uk

Whose details should be included?

Residents aged 16+ who are:


  *   British or Irish citizens
  *   Qualifying EU citizens (Denmark, Luxembourg, Poland, Portugal, Spain)
  *   EU citizens with retained rights (living in the UK before 31 Dec 2020=
)
  *   Commonwealth citizens with permission to stay in the UK


If unsure, check: Which elections can I vote in?<https://www.electoralcommi=
ssion.org.uk/i-am-a/voter/which-elections-can-i-vote>

If you do not qualify, you still need to respond to tell us that you do not=
 qualify. Otherwise, we will continue to ask you for information.


  *   Students =96 Students may register at both their home address and ter=
m time address but can only vote once in the same election.
  *   Second home owners =96 Second home owners can register at more than o=
ne address, but only if they are resident in both and split their time equa=
lity between the two addresses.

Whose details should not be included?


  *   Residents at the address who are under 16 years of age
  *   Residents who are not British, Irish, European Union or Commonwealth =
citizens
  *   Visitors staying at the address at the time the canvass communication=
 is responded to
  *   Landlords who do not live at the address.


I am already registered to vote. Do I need to respond?

If the communication you receive indicates that a response is required, you=
 must respond.
This includes confirmation of NO CHANGE to the information we already hold =
for your property. Please respond as requested when we contact you.

You must respond to the canvass communication if:


  *   Any information is incorrect, or there are people eligible to registe=
r to vote at your address who are not included, OR
  *   The notification you receive states that you must respond


Some or all the current occupiers are not yet registered. How can they regi=
ster?

All new residents at your address must apply to register to vote. Applicati=
ons should be submitted as soon as possible. The quickest and easiest way t=
o make an application to register to vote is by visiting www.gov.uk/registe=
r-to-vote<http://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote>

Where new names are provided to us, a registration form will be issued by e=
mail or post.

Individuals will only be added to the electoral register if they either sub=
mit an online application or complete and return the form provided.
Details of register publication dates and registration deadlines can be fou=
nd at Voting and elections | Southwark Council<https://www.southwark.gov.uk=
/about-council/voting-and-elections>

How do I remove people who no longer live at my address? (for example, if t=
hey have moved out)

To make a change, visit householdresponse.southwark.gov.uk<https://househol=
dresponse.southwark.gov.uk/>


  *   Enter both parts of the security code provided on the canvass communi=
cation
  *   Remove the names of the people who should no longer be registered at =
your address


I have changed my name. What should I do?

To change your name on the electoral register, you will need to provide evi=
dence of your name change, for example a marriage certificate or deed poll =
certificate.

Please email a copy of your evidence, alongside details of your address and=
 how you would like your name to appear to electoral.enquiries@southwark.go=
v.uk<mailto:electoral.enquiries@southwark.gov.uk>

There is a mistake in the address on the form. Can this be changed?

Please email electoral.enquiries@southwark.gov.uk<mailto:electoral.enquirie=
s@southwark.gov.uk> with your name, address and the error.

We will investigate and amend if necessary. Any change will take effect on =
publication of the revised electoral register on 1 December 2026.

I am about to move. What should I do?

As soon as you move, apply to register at your new address by visiting www.=
gov.uk/register-to-vote<http://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote>

When making your application you must provide your old address =96 this wil=
l make sure that your name is not on the register in two places at the same=
 time.

Why is the canvass communication not addressed to me?

The purpose of the communication is to identify who should be registered to=
 vote at an address. It is addressed to =91Resident=92 because the person o=
r people that we have registered at an address may no longer be there.

I am paying Council Tax. Why am I not registered to vote?

Paying Council Tax does not mean you are automatically added to the Elector=
al Register. Not everyone who pays Council Tax is eligible to be registered=
, and not everyone who is eligible to vote at an address will be named on t=
he Council Tax account.

By law, each individual is responsible for making their own application to =
register to vote.

How do I change my method of voting? (apply for or cancel a postal or proxy=
 vote)


  *   You can apply for a postal vote<https://www.gov.uk/apply-postal-vote>=
 or for a proxy vote<https://www.gov.uk/how-to-vote/voting-by-proxy> online=
.
  *   To cancel an existing postal or proxy vote, please email electoral.en=
quiries@southwark.gov.uk<mailto:electoral.enquiries@southwark.gov.uk>

You cannot cancel a postal or proxy application on someone else=92s behalf =
- the request must come from the individual concerned.

I do not vote so I don=92t want to register=85

It is not a legal requirement to vote but the law requires eligible residen=
ts to register when requested to do so.

There are benefits of being on the register that are unconnected with elect=
ions. Credit referencing agencies are entitled to receive the electoral reg=
ister and use the information contained in it as part of an individual=92s =
credit records, using it as verification of their address.

If someone is not on the electoral register it may affect their ability to =
obtain credit, get a mortgage or access other financial services.
What is the open register?

There are two versions of the electoral register =96 the full version and t=
he =91open register=92:

The full register
Everyone=92s name and address is included on the full version of the electo=
ral register. The full version of the register is only used for:


  *   electoral purposes (including campaigning by political parties)
  *   preventing and detecting crime
  *   used by credit reference agencies to check credit applications
  *
jury summoning in England, Wales and Northern Ireland

The open register
This is an extract of the electoral register, which is not used for electio=
ns. Any person, company or organisation can buy a copy of this register. Fo=
r example, it can be used by businesses and charities to confirm name and a=
ddress details or for mailing lists.

You may choose to have your name removed from the open register. Removing y=
our details from the open register does not affect your right to vote.

The canvass communication will indicate the registered voters who have opte=
d out of the open register.

How do I remove my name from the open register?

You can ask for your name and address to be excluded from the Open Register=
 on a permanent basis. You can make your request by emailing electoral.enqu=
ries@southwark.gov.uk<mailto:electoral.enquries@southwark.gov.uk>


  *   you cannot do this on behalf of another person
  *   each individual must inform the Electoral Registration Officer separa=
tely that they want to do this


Why do you write to my property every year?

The Electoral Registration Officer is required by law to carry out a househ=
old canvass each year to determine who should be registered to vote. This i=
ncludes not only identifying where changes need to be made, but also confir=
ming where there is no change needed to registered elector details.

I have responded to the form already. Why have received a reminder form?

It takes several days to print, dispatch and deliver reminder forms. There =
is always a cross-over period after data for the reminder forms is sent to =
our print contractors and the forms are finally delivered. Your response ma=
y not have been received by us when we sent the reminder data to our printe=
rs.

For more information about the electoral register and voter registration, p=
lease visit the Electoral Commission=92s website<https://www.electoralcommi=
ssion.org.uk/i-am-a/voter>.





The email you received and any files transmitted with it are confidential, =
may be covered by legal and/or professional privilege and are intended sole=
ly for the use of the individual or entity to whom they are addressed. If y=
ou have received this in error please notify us immediately. If you are not=
 the intended recipient of the email or the person responsible for deliveri=
ng it to them you may not copy it, forward it or otherwise use it for any p=
urpose or disclose its contents to any other person. To do so may be unlawf=
ul. Where opinions are expressed in the email they are not necessarily thos=
e of Southwark Council and Southwark Council is not responsible for any cha=
nges made to the message after it has been sent.

